{
 "$comment": "Authoritative cases for the page's own account of where a tap landed - read by BOTH the Kotlin (reticle-core DomTapWitnessContractTest) and Swift (ReticleProtocol DomTapWitnessContractTests) suites, so the Android helper and the iOS host cannot judge a missed tap two different ways. Everything else a tap reports is what it INTENDED; this is the one fact that comes from the page. Measured on a real hybrid page whose DOM rects were offset by roughly 130px, where a --css tap missed twice, reported settled=1 both times, and only a screenshot revealed it (#234). What the cases pin: the two shapes that must stay QUIET (the element aimed at, and anything inside it - a button's caption span is that button), the three that must be reported (another element, the containing element, a pointer that matched nothing captured), the difference between a touch that went elsewhere and NO touch at all, and the four cases that are refused rather than guessed: an unreadable page, a native target, a target inside a frame the witness cannot be installed in, and a record too old to belong to this gesture.",
